
This directory is intended for PlatformIO Test Runner and project tests.

Unit Testing is a software testing method by which individual units of
source code, sets of one or more MCU program modules together with associated
control data, usage procedures, and operating procedures, are tested to
determine whether they are fit for use. Unit testing finds problems early
in the development cycle.

More information about PlatformIO Unit Testing:
- https://docs.platformio.org/en/latest/advanced/unit-testing/index.html

GPS/RTC timestamp regression tests (native only):

  python test\test_rtc_sync\test_rtc_sync.py

  Compiles production RTC synchronization functions and CSV timestamp formatting
  with a mocked clock. Requires the installed Python ziglang C++17 compiler.
  Covers repeated/out-of-order GPS times, the +/-1 second
  whole-second GPS tolerance, larger corrections, unset/invalid RTC, timezone and
  DST changes, invalid fields, manual adjustment, 3000 simulated 10 Hz samples,
  and coherent seconds/milliseconds across midnight, year and leap-day rollovers.
  The source-wiring test checks that synchronization follows a fresh accepted GPS
  fix, not the 100 ms logging scheduler.

GPS freshness and SIM7600 scheduling tests (native only):

  python test\test_gps_scheduler\test_gps_scheduler.py

  Compiles the production GPS freshness and command-timeout helpers with native
  state. Verifies cellular attach/activation retains its 30-second timeout,
  DNS retains 12 seconds, MQTT connect retains 90 seconds, and prompt/publish
  stages use their configured timeouts. Also verifies stale fixes clear cached
  coordinates after the grace period and millis rollover is handled.

  The old scheduler repeatedly reapplied cached whole-second GPS time and could
  rewind the RTC/reset milliseconds, producing different measurement rows with
  the same timestamp. Fresh GPS fixes now synchronize once per advancing UTC
  second; small whole-second differences no longer reset the subsecond phase.
  CSV logging also uses one RTC epoch/subsecond snapshot rather than independent
  field reads. Existing logs are unchanged. Deliberate large clock corrections,
  manual changes and DST transitions can still move local wall time backward;
  Cortex does not hide or discard measurement rows.

  COM18 hardware check, 2026-10-27: uploaded the signed ZIP over authenticated
  USB serial and recorded/downloaded a fresh log. All 2159 measurement rows
  had unique, increasing timestamps; 1621 rows had valid GPS coordinates,
  spanning approximately 162 seconds with 82-118 ms intervals after GPS lock.
  No duplicate full rows occurred. Initial GPS acquisition legitimately advanced
  the RTC by about 29 seconds; the near-second tolerance did not suppress this
  real correction. The encrypted bulk download passed framing/checksum checks
  (1225385 bytes; SHA256
  d37a7b598688d59c8b7458b1696a8a270d35011cf790122dc12d8b03a02ef78e).
  The PIN remained enabled; Status/Lock succeeded and COM18 was closed afterward.

PIN lock and encrypted Cortex transport tests (native only):

test_pin_lock compiles the production PinLock.cpp, CortexPort.cpp and installed
Crypto primitives, with only the serial/time/storage hardware mocked. It has
its own native main() and is excluded from STM32_test. Do not flash it.
The RNG stub deliberately aborts on use of the library's unseeded platform RNG.
Long-command coverage simulates a three-minute provisioning handler with and
without a PIN, verifies its reply and subsequent configuration request, and
checks the five-second idle timeout starts again at command completion.

Use a GCC/Clang-compatible C++17 compiler (or "zig c++") with:
  include paths: test\test_pin_lock\mocks and .pio\libdeps\STM32\Crypto\src
  project sources: src\PinLock.cpp src\CortexPort.cpp test\test_pin_lock\test_main.cpp
  Crypto sources: Crypto.cpp Hash.cpp SHA256.cpp HKDF.cpp Curve25519.cpp
    BigNumberUtil.cpp ChaChaPoly.cpp AuthenticatedCipher.cpp Cipher.cpp
    ChaCha.cpp Poly1305.cpp

Compile all listed sources and run the resulting executable. No extra test
framework or device is required. MSVC alone cannot compile Crypto's GNU
statement-expression rotation macros.

Coverage includes an independently generated Python PBKDF2/salt/recovery vector,
PIN format and privilege checks, retry thresholds and millis wraparound,
microsecond hashing budgets/wraparound and stalled-clock round limits,
cached HMAC prefixes checked against the reference KDF across PIN changes,
disconnect/aborted-verification/reboot retry persistence, recovery replacement,
rotation/disable, corrupt storage and write failure failing closed, default
legacy compatibility, the locked plaintext allowlist, encrypted unlock and
responses, plaintext bypass attempts after unlock, replay and ciphertext/tag
tampering, fragmented/truncated/oversized envelopes, session expiry/explicit
locking, bounded transmit backpressure, unique handshake IDs without repeated
EEPROM writes, authorized plaintext log output without changing encryption or
sequence counters for other replies, locked/expired plaintext log rejection,
plaintext log transmit backpressure,
no recovery readback, and factory erasure preserving the exact security region with a
destructive mock SPI EEPROM driver.

Wire formats, the Cortex pairing/identity requirements and hardware limitations
are in Documentation\PIN-lock-protocol.txt. Build firmware with "pio run -e STM32".
Bench checks in that document are still required before deployment.

COM18 plaintext log / DTR A-B benchmark, 2026-10-27:

  Changed only the CheckSerial disconnect test from !Serial to !Serial.dtr().
  USBSerial::operator bool() in the installed STM32 core delays 10 ms; dtr()
  returns the same DTR state without that delay. Uploaded the signed ZIP over
  authenticated USB serial. PIN remained enabled.
  The same closed 884713-byte log, with plaintext bulk output after PIN unlock,
  took 5.9597/5.9593 seconds before the change (144.97/144.98 KiB/s) and
  1.4670/1.4650 seconds after it (588.93/589.73 KiB/s), using the same buffered
  receiver. Median packet gaps fell from 12.8 ms to 2.8 ms; gaps above 10 ms
  fell from 432/433 packets to zero. All download lengths and SHA-256 values
  matched 994774ff54f199995021fcad9c29acee6b4c22b854b73f5ad58c9a8e4fdaf0af.
  An additional exact-read receiver completed in 1.472 seconds (586.9 KiB/s).
  PIN unlock wall time fell from approximately 3.3 seconds to 1.14 seconds.
  Encrypted Status/Lock succeeded after plaintext output, and COM18 was closed.
  This confirms the blocking check was the dominant current slowdown, but does
  not explain the remaining difference from the historical 704 KiB/s result
  on a different file/build. Hardware disconnect timing is not qualified here.

  Follow-up, same flashed firmware and buffered receiver: the 884713-byte file
  completed in 1.4648/1.4664 seconds (589.83/589.16 KiB/s). A closed
  20450328-byte log completed in 32.1084/32.1025 seconds (621.99/622.10 KiB/s),
  with repeat SHA-256
  00418374c00cacfec26af8fc5883b6deb5f3be21122c16964a4658a35c040f06.
  No packet gaps exceeded 10 ms in any of these four runs.
  Download I/O service count increased by 1342; observed download maxima were
  4521 us per service and 58159 us start-to-start. ADC batch failures remained
  zero. These counters do not measure total SD-read or USB-write time and do
  not qualify loaded fault response. No further firmware/settings changes
  were made during these follow-up measurements.

