    884713 bytes: before 1.4648/1.4664 s, after 1.2058/1.2032 s;
                 589 KiB/s -> 717 KiB/s, 433 -> 432 packets.
    20450328 bytes: before 32.1084/32.1025 s, after 26.5641/26.5628 s;
                   622 KiB/s -> 752 KiB/s, 9991 -> 9986 packets.

  Both after-runs matched the pre-change SHA-256 values recorded above.
  Every nonfinal packet contained exactly 2048 bytes, final progress was 100,
  and every additive checksum passed. Throughput improved approximately 21%.
  Download I/O service count increased by 1110; observed download maxima
  were 4640 us per service and 56321 us start-to-start. ADC failures stayed
  zero; these observations do not qualify loaded fault response.
  Encrypted pull and encrypted bulk were also verified on the 884713-byte
  file: 2.4664 s / 350.3 KiB/s and 1.8049 s / 478.7 KiB/s respectively.
  Both matched the original hash; all authentication tags/checksums passed.
  Session locked and COM18 closed. No SD-clock, configuration, sampling or
  protection-schedule changes were made.

COM18 Cortex plaintext download cancellation verification, 2026-10-27:

  Fixed the desktop client's cancellation path without changing/flashing firmware.
  The bulk reader owns cancellation: finish the current packet, send protected
  LOG_CANCEL, drain queued bulk packets/pending Status, authenticate the cancel
  ACK and only then return the serial reader to normal polling. Redundant
  screen cleanup cancels are ignored after a completed/cancelled bulk transfer.
  Failed cleanup remains bounded to five seconds and requires reconnecting;
  ordinary cancellation preserves session keys, sequences and authorization.

  Actual Cortex SerialPortService on COM18 passed four cancel/restart cycles,
  exercising token cancellation, direct CancelLogTransfer, cancellation after
  a keepalive interval and cancellation on the final packet. ACK/drain times
  were 11, 14, 12 and 1 ms. Each cycle resumed live updates, opened the other
  closed log and downloaded its complete 884713 bytes with the original hash.
  The original large file could also be reopened/cancelled. Session ID remained
  unchanged throughout; no reconnect or second PIN unlock was performed.
  Protected Status remained authorized, then explicit Lock succeeded and the
  port closed. No parameter writes, log deletion or firmware upload occurred.

  Cortex build and 62 focused serial/cancellation tests passed, including the
  opt-in hardware test. Unit cases cover cancellation inside headers/payloads/
  checksums, queued packets, final-packet/pending-Status races, repeated cancel
  requests, legacy raw ACKs, tampered/negative ACKs and missing ACK rejection.
  The real-client workflow was exercised, not GUI automation.

COM18 PIN-unlock bench result, 2026-10-27: cached HMAC prefixes and a 5 ms
hashing slice (maximum 256 rounds) reduced unlock from 8.73-8.77 seconds to
3.313, 3.316, 3.356, 3.366 and 3.431 seconds in five repeated checks on the
final signed firmware (median 3.356 seconds, approximately 62% faster).
All five were below the 4.5-second bench acceptance threshold and returned
valid static configuration packets. The existing PIN, 10,000 PBKDF2 rounds,
encrypted transport and retry policy were unchanged; no PIN migration is needed.
Live/static, math, digital-input, custom-CAN, controller and ADC replies passed
encryption/sequence/checksum checks, as did 50 consecutive live reads, locked
plaintext rejection, explicit Lock and five-second session expiry. I/O and ADC
counters advanced across re-authentication without new acquisition errors.
Temporary timing instrumentation and display/ignition scheduling experiments
were removed before the final build. There is no final display or power-state
behavior change. These measurements do not establish loaded electrical fault
response, worst-case I/O deadlines, or end-to-end Cortex GUI behavior.

Custom CAN and math tests:

  pio test -e STM32_test -f test_custom_can -f test_math

This command uploads test firmware; use only a disconnected bench controller.
To compile both suites without accessing hardware:

  pio test -e STM32_test -f test_custom_can -f test_math --without-uploading --without-testing

test_custom_can also has a native main() when ARDUINO is not defined. It can be
compiled with a desktop C++ compiler and Unity's unity.c, using the Unity src
directory as an include path. It requires no CAN hardware or Arduino stubs.
Coverage includes the 100-slot capacity, shared frames, both DBC byte orders,
cross-byte and 1-64-bit signed/unsigned decoding, invalid configurations and
frames, exact timeout boundaries, the 60-second ceiling, timestamp wraparound,
cache invalidation, parameter read/write validation, and float overflow.
It also covers whole-record serial payloads (including little-endian extended
IDs and floats), atomic rejection, relocation without invalid intermediate
states, exact payload lengths, clearing defaults without cascading to shared
signals, and unchanged writes preserving runtime values and dirty state.

test_math includes a shared custom CAN input used by two math channels, missing
and expired source errors, recovery, and rejection of signal index 100.

On-device validation is still required for EEPROM save/reboot/CRC corruption
and receive-queue behavior under the intended bus load. EEPROM CRC failure
must disable the custom bank without damaging legacy or math records.

test_eeprom_blocks exercises the production page writer with a fake M95640R
that overwrites its input buffer, matching the real driver's in-place SPI
transfers. It verifies source preservation, page boundaries, unaligned CAN
bank writes, headers/checksums, and empty writes. Like test_custom_can, it
supports a native Unity main() and the STM32_test environment.
Compare-before-write coverage verifies all 100 signal positions: each changed
signal requires at most two payload writes plus a CRC write, versus 66 write
cycles for an entirely changed bank. Unchanged banks require zero writes.
Tests also cover repair of unrelated EEPROM corruption and adjacent-byte
preservation. Full-bank readback verification remains in SaveCustomCANConfig.

Compile these focused suites without uploading:

  pio test -e STM32_test -f test_custom_can -f test_eeprom_blocks --without-uploading --without-testing

On the bench, also check fragmented/back-to-back serial frames, c/f responses,
save/reboot persistence, failed-write retry, and actual Cortex save/delete
latency. Native tests measure EEPROM write counts, not wall-clock hardware time.
The serial-only record/clear protocol and recommended one-save-per-edit-batch
flow are documented in src/canwiki.txt under "Cortex serial interface".

ADC batch sampler tests (native only):

This mock-HAL suite is excluded from the STM32_test environment.
Compile src/AdcSampler.cpp and test/test_adc_sampler/test_main.cpp with Unity,
putting test/test_adc_sampler/mocks before the Arduino include paths. For example,
with a native C/C++ compiler (compile unity.c as C before linking):

  cc -I .pio/libdeps/STM32_test/Unity/src -c .pio/libdeps/STM32_test/Unity/src/unity.c -o unity.o
  c++ -std=c++17 -I test/test_adc_sampler/mocks -I .pio/libdeps/STM32_test/Unity/src src/AdcSampler.cpp test/test_adc_sampler/test_main.cpp unity.o -o adc-tests

Eight tests exercise the production synchronous batch sampler: 100 distinct
conversions with one initialisation, single samples on each sense pin,
full-scale accumulator range, conversion/setup/start/cleanup failures,
invalid requests, and I/O/download timing including timestamp wraparound.
Failed acquisitions never publish a mean; the output handler shuts off and
locks a digital output if its batch acquisition fails.

Downloads skip TFT redraws, but retain the existing I/O service schedule,
input/math evaluation, battery/thermal protection and CAN processing.
The read-only serial command '?' returns the normal framed response with a
40-byte payload: ten little-endian uint32 values in this order:
  batch count, completed samples, failed batches, maximum batch time (us),
  I/O services, maximum I/O duration (us), maximum I/O start gap (us),
  download I/O services, maximum download I/O duration (us),
  maximum gap between consecutive download I/O services (us).
Counters reset on boot and maxima include all observations since boot. Do not
inject diagnostic commands into an active raw bulk stream; query before/after.

Hardware qualification must compare current readings under load, PWM/ramp
behaviour, input-to-output and fault-to-shutoff latency during download, and
sampling failures. Native tests do not establish electrical fault response.
Firmware builds produce a signed .zip containing .bin, .sig and .sha256;
normally install that package through Cortex's USB update workflow. For
authorised ST-Link recovery, program only the application at 0x08010000;
preserve the bootloader and EEPROM configuration.

COM18 bench result, 2026-10-25: actual Cortex ReadLogBulkAsync downloaded the
same 20,389,822-byte file in 32.86 s versus 351.05 s before this change, and the
same 5,840,452-byte file in 8.51 s versus 99.45 s. Both files matched their
original SHA-256 hashes; each new measurement was repeated. The bench had 11
steady enabled digital outputs with zero reported load current. Timing counters
showed I/O continuing during download, a maximum observed service duration of
4.537 ms and start-to-start gap of 58.235 ms, with zero batch acquisition errors.
These are observed bench maxima, not guaranteed protection-response bounds.
The SDIO divider and normal I/O schedule were unchanged; ADC batching and skipped
download TFT redraws were both enabled. PWM/load/fault-injection qualification
remains required before treating these measurements as production validation.

CURRENT ACQUISITION: ADC DMA rollback (supersedes all DMA notes below)

The STM32 build now uses synchronous acquisition only. Steady, fully-on digital
outputs collect 100 distinct ADC1 conversions per mean with one HAL setup and
teardown (one conversion during configuration saves). PWM and digital ramps,
analogue inputs, temperature, reference and battery use ordinary analogRead.
The previous PWM coefficients/filter/protection behavior has been restored;
the DMA full-cycle current calibration is not applicable and has been removed.
There are no ADC DMA buffers, interrupts, trigger timers or runtime ADC modes.
The existing GPIO PWM generator still uses DMA, as before the ADC experiment.
DMA-only bench commands '=', 'W' and '@' have been removed; '?' remains.
The SD recovery recursion fix, skipped download TFT redraws, original I/O
schedule, watchdog and unrelated custom CAN/math support are retained.
The SDIO divider is unchanged. No card formatting or configuration reset is
part of this rollback. Reset stability, SD health and channel 7's unloaded
current must be rechecked on hardware; the historical DMA tests below do not
qualify this firmware.

