HISTORICAL ONLY: all-channel ADC DMA experiment (removed):

  pio run -e STM32

All firmware environments use DMA unconditionally. The alternate ADC build
environment and acquisition feature flag have been removed, along with the
analogRead and synchronous batch fallback paths. Firmware has been uploaded
to COM18 through the signed USB ZIP updater, with outputs isolated before
each installation. PWM mean calibration is shared by all 14 channels;
channel 7 remains unvalidated because of the protection trip recorded below.
Loaded protection and transient response remain unqualified.
Use the signed ZIP in
.pio\build\STM32 through Cortex's USB updater only after bench review.
Do not use PlatformIO's default ST-Link upload target.

The DMA path owns all three ADCs for their entire awake lifetime. No analogRead,
HAL_ADC_DeInit, or synchronous current batch may run alongside it:
  ADC1: PC4 battery, internal temperature and VREFINT, TIM3 TRGO at 1 kHz,
        DMA2 stream 4/channel 0, 480-cycle sampling.
  ADC2: all 14 current-sense pins in channelCurrentSensePins order, TIM1 CC1
        every 66.5 us, DMA2 stream 2/channel 1, 15-cycle sampling.
  ADC3: PF3..PF10 analogue inputs, TIM3 TRGO at 1 kHz,
        DMA2 stream 0/channel 2, 84-cycle sampling.
All use 12-bit conversion and PCLK2 / 4. DMA2 streams 1 and 5 remain reserved
for PWM GPIO writes. PB10 backlight PWM remains on TIM2; TIM3 is reserved for
slow ADC scans in this environment. ADC/DMA IRQ priorities are 5.
TIM5 is reserved as a free-running 1 MHz acquisition clock. Arduino micros()
can move backwards around a pending SysTick while interrupts are masked; it
must not be used for acquisition freshness or DMA callback timestamps.

TIM1 enable/TRGO starts TIM8 through ITR0. Both retain the existing 100-slot,
approximately 150 Hz waveform. The first update writes GPIO slot zero, then
TIM1 CC1 in PWM2 mode triggers current scans 32 us into each slot. Frozen
output-compare mode did not generate ADC2 acquisitions on the COM18 hardware.
Fourteen conversions
nominally take 18 us at the configured ADC clock. Circular DMA stores two full
PWM cycles (5600 bytes), with one completed half processed per cycle.

For the diagnostic on-window mean/maximum, slots 0..4 and the final on-slot
are excluded. The first retained
sample starts approximately 364.5 us after the commanded rising edge; this is
an INITIAL BLANKING ASSUMPTION, not a measured current-sense settling guarantee.
Every retained on-window sample contributes to the integer window mean.
At 100% duty all 100 samples are used. Bench waveform acquisition established
that channel 1's ADC signal is already PWM-averaged: at 50% it varies only a few
counts across both on/off portions. The full 100-slot floating-point raw mean
now drives telemetry; multiplying that signal by duty again was incorrect.
This is not an RMS current or measurement of inductive recirculation current
outside the high-side switch. Other channels' analogue response still requires
qualification; the shared calibration does not establish transient accuracy.

Intentional, opt-in behaviour changes:
  - Any nonzero duty below 10% (including soft ramps) is raised to 10%, because
    shorter pulses do not have the configured settled measurement window.
  - A duty edit invalidates the intersecting cycle. Only a full subsequent
    stable cycle can publish current. While settling, the last valid displayed
    current is retained, AnalogRaw is -1, and CHN_CURRENT_STALE (0x40) is set.
    That held display value is never used for overcurrent protection.
  - Maximum valid window raw reading is retained across cycles until the
    output service consumes it. This is NOT instantaneous on-current, because
    the signal is already filtered in the acquisition path. Protection uses
    the larger of the two pre-existing raw-to-current conversions, divided by
    actual duty, as a conservative on-current estimate, without display EMA.
    This also applies during ramps/soft-stop. It cannot recover short-circuit
    transients removed by analogue filtering. Sense-fault voltage checks still
    use the measured signal. Current limits are not increased. Trips latch
    RETRY_LOCKOUT until the channel is disabled; they do not automatically retry.
    Existing digital three-pass checks remain as secondary checks, while PWM
    low-current checks retain their displayed-mean basis.
  - ADC/DMA errors, buffer overwrite, unavailable channels, zero VREFINT,
    stopped PWM timers and stale acquisition fail closed. ADC_ERROR (system
    bit 0x0200) reports acquisition failure; the PWM DMA requests/timers are
    stopped and GPIO outputs cleared. Recovery requires acquisition restart
    on a subsequent wake or reboot. Retained telemetry is explicitly stale,
    not a successful zero-current or zero-voltage measurement.
  - Read freshness is limited to 20 ms. Duty-change settling must also finish
    within 20 ms. Input/system readings are latest completed 1 ms scans.
    Sleep stops acquisition and wake rebuilds ownership before input evaluation.

The '?' response remains exactly 40 bytes. In DMA builds its first four values
count completed DMA halves (plus failure records), converted samples, latched
acquisition failures, and maximum DMA-half processing time. I/O fields retain
their original meanings. Counts are copied atomically for the response.

Bench serial commands:
  '=' returns a normal framed packet containing 17 little-endian uint32 values:
      first fault reason, acquisition-clock timestamp, TIM1 CR1, TIM8 CR1,
      current DMA NDTR, ADC2 SR, DMA2 LISR, DMA2 HISR, ADC2 CR2, TIM1 CCR1,
      TIM1 CCMR1, TIM1 CCER, TIM1 CNT, TIM1 SR, TIM1 ARR, ADC2 SQR1,
      live PWM test status.
      The final word packs actual selected-channel duty in bits 0..7, fixed test duty
      in bits 8..15, and the 1-based test channel in bits 16..23.
      With no test channel selected, the actual duty is channel 1's.
      Fault reason zero means no latched acquisition failure.
  'W' followed by two raw bytes (1-based channel, physical duty) selects a
      volatile fixed-duty test override of battery compensation. Channel zero
      clears it. Nonzero duty must be 10..100%; zero is also permitted.
      Response is c/f. This command does NOT enable a channel, alter current
      thresholds, clear lockouts, or disable acquisition protection. It applies
      only to the selected channel's PWM path. Wake/reboot clears the test
      override; the persisted configured duty uses normal compensation again.
      It does not expire on serial disconnect, so a user can check their load.
      Isolate other channels and verify the applied duty/status before testing.
  '@' returns a normal framed 208-byte payload for the channel selected by W
      (channel 1 when no override is selected): uint32 TIM5
      timestamp, uint32 applied duty, then 100 little-endian uint16 ADC samples
      in PWM-slot order. It snapshots the latest complete cycle without
      consuming protection peak history. Failure returns f and fails closed.

COM18 bring-up found and corrected two issues before enabling the load:
frozen TIM1 compare produced no ADC2 trigger, and SysTick-derived timestamps
caused false stale-data faults. With PWM2 and TIM5, complete current scans and
zero acquisition failures were observed with outputs isolated. During the
initial compensated-duty load trial, the user observed approximately 3 A on
the PDM and 4.1 A on the electronic load (2 ohm setting). That is approximately
27% low, but is NOT a calibration result: exact applied duty and the load's
mean/RMS reporting must be established first. Supply voltage changed under
load, motivating the explicit fixed-physical-duty bench command.
Initial fixed-duty bench build: v0.11, Oct 25 2026 22:06:51. Serial readback
confirmed channel 1 at exactly 50% applied duty (test word 0x00013232), with
channels 2..14 disabled and their control sources isolated. The configured
duty is also 50%; reboot clears the fixed-duty override and resumes voltage
compensation. Original configuration was backed up before test isolation.
At the first fixed-duty snapshot the PDM reported approximately 1.92 A and
15.4 V. This has NOT been reconciled with an external current measurement.
After more than seven million acquired samples the failure counter remained
zero. Existing current thresholds were not increased. Acquisition health
does not establish current calibration or loaded protection accuracy.

Read-only Tenma comparison, COM6, same fixed-50% configuration:
  ID: TENMA 72-13210 V3.30 SN:00022848, 115200 baud, 8N1.
  Queries: *IDN?, :RES?, :MEAS:VOLT?, :MEAS:CURR?, :MEAS:POW?.
  Resistance readback: 2.0000 ohms. Twelve paired samples gave:
    Tenma voltage 7.70369 V, current 3.647475 A, power 28.09725 W;
    PDM supply 15.44167 V, reported current 1.9279835 A.
  The PDM reading was 47.14% below the Tenma current (ratio 1.89186).
  Channel 1 stayed enabled without channel faults; channels 2..14 stayed off.
  No load settings, firmware, duty or calibration were changed for these reads.
  The load voltage is approximately half the supply, consistent with a
  cycle-averaged voltage reading. Its power is approximately voltage readback
  times current readback, so it is not independent proof of true PWM power.
  The PDM pre-duty-scaled estimate (approximately 3.856 A) is much closer to
  the load current. This suggests the assumption that settled ADC readings
  represent instantaneous on-current must be checked against the actual
  current-sense analogue response/calibration. It does not justify removing
  duty scaling or applying a one-point gain correction without waveform tests.

Native DMA arithmetic/state-boundary tests (no hardware, not an upload target):

  cc -I .pio\libdeps\STM32_test\Unity\src -c .pio\libdeps\STM32_test\Unity\src\unity.c -o unity.o
  c++ -std=c++17 -I .pio\libdeps\STM32_test\Unity\src test\test_dma_adc\test_main.cpp unity.o -o dma-adc-tests

These fourteen tests execute the production reducer, conversion and timing/buffer guards. They
cover all 14 scan ranks, every supported duty, edge/off-time rejection, varying
pulse averages, full-cycle fractional mean, filtered-peak protection estimates,
shared calibration on all 14 ranks and held-out Tenma regression fixtures,
full-scale accumulation, invalid requests, overwritten halves, freshness and timer wrap.
They do NOT simulate the HAL, prove timer wiring, or establish electrical
protection timing. The three DMA diagnostics tests also remain applicable.

Required hardware qualification before deployment:
  1. Scope TIM1/TIM8-driven GPIO edges and ADC trigger timing on both port groups;
     verify each scan rank stays within its valid window at 10%, 50%, 95%, 100%.
  2. Measure sense settling/turn-off tails and recalibrate current with resistive
     and inductive loads, including soft-start/stop and duty changes.
  3. Inject ADC/DMA faults and interrupt delays; confirm physical shutdown,
     latched status, stale telemetry, sleep/wake and restart behaviour.
  4. Measure loaded overcurrent/fault-to-shutoff and input-to-output latency while
     downloading logs and saving configuration. Peak retention prevents a valid
     sampled spike being lost between I/O passes, but overcurrent decisions
     STILL run on the existing main-loop I/O schedule (normally 50 ms).
     This is not cycle-by-cycle shutdown; filtered ADC measurements cannot
     qualify switching transient protection. Hardware driver protection remains vital.
  5. Re-run Cortex downloads and check hashes, ISR overhead, stack/heap headroom
     and RAM use. The calibrated DMA bench build uses 116260 bytes static RAM (88.7%),
     leaving roughly 15 KiB for stack/heap. No new speedup is claimed without
     benchmarking this build on hardware.

Original channel-1 PWM calibration experiment, 2026-10-25 (historical):

  Fit data: Tenma CR at 16, 8, 4 ohms, with physical duty
  0, 10, 20, 35, 50, 65, 80, 95, 100 percent; five paired samples/point.
  ADC waveform readback was captured for each pair. Zero/low-signal points
  below five ADC counts are excluded from the linear fit and retain the
  existing noise-floor clamp. No resistance-to-current idealisation was used;
  the reference is the Tenma's measured current, not V/R or its power readback.

  Channel 1 mean amps = 0.0152108531 * full-cycle ADC mean + 0.0182784096.
  Duty zero or raw mean below five counts reports zero. No second duty scaling.
  Coefficients are compiled in src/PWMCurrent.h for this bench channel only,
  survive reboot/USB reinstall, and do not overwrite digital KILIS calibration.
  Other PWM channels use their stored individual KILIS conversion without
  the extra duty multiplier; they are explicitly not calibrated by this test.

  Held-out validation after USB installation used 12, 6, 3 ohms and duties
  0, 15, 25, 40, 55, 70, 90, 100 percent. These resistances were not fit data.
  All 24 point averages met max(0.10 A, 5% of reference). Mean absolute error
  was 0.04071 A; worst absolute error was 0.10557 A at approximately 5.015 A.
  Example reference/PDM pairs:
    12 ohms, 55%: 0.60858 / 0.62917 A
     6 ohms, 55%: 1.32072 / 1.31069 A
     3 ohms, 55%: 2.69770 / 2.62909 A
     3 ohms,100%: 5.01480 / 5.12037 A
  At very low current the five-count clamp can report zero for a nonzero load:
  12 ohms/15% gave approximately 0.063 A on the Tenma. No sub-0.1 A accuracy
  is claimed. Near the lower limit, absolute rather than percentage error
  dominates. Settling between settings was 2.2 seconds; these tests do not
  establish fast transient response.

  Both port groups remained isolated except channel 1, and limits were kept.
  Sweeps always ended at zero duty and 16 ohms. Final manual comparison uses
  4 ohms/50%, not the original 2 ohms: 2 ohms can exceed the existing 7 A
  on-current limit at this supply. Never increase that limit just to calibrate.
  Final installed build: Oct 25 2026 22:21:53. Twelve paired readings at
  4 ohms/physical 50% averaged 1.81899 A Tenma and 1.77698 A PDM (2.31% low).
  Only channel 1 was enabled, with no channel faults, no latched DMA fault
  and zero acquisition failures after 5.8 million samples. System error 0x0040
  was the GPS warning, not an ADC fault. Both serial ports were closed afterward.
  The fixed-duty override is volatile: reboot resumes configured,
  voltage-compensated PWM rather than guaranteeing physical 50%.
  This is empirical agreement with this Tenma under these conditions, not
  traceable metrology, all-channel calibration or loaded protection approval.

Cross-channel transfer check, 2026-10-25 (before global application):

  With the outputs externally tied to the same Tenma load, only one channel
  was enabled at a time. The existing channel-1 coefficients were applied
  OFFLINE to each selected channel's full-cycle ADC waveform, without changing
  its firmware calibration or current limits. W now selects the channel for
  both waveform readback and the actual-duty diagnostic.
  Tests used 16, 8 and 4 ohms at physical duties 25, 50, 75 and 100 percent,
  five paired samples/point and 2.2 seconds settling. All outputs were disabled
  and load current checked between channels. This shared-output fixture is
  not representative of independent-load fault behavior.

  Channels 1-5, 8 and 10-14 passed all 12 points each. Channel 6 passed its
  four 16-ohm points; its existing 2 A limit excluded heavier loads.
  Channel 9 passed eight points at 16/8 ohms; its 5 A limit and the test's
  conservative supply/current margin excluded 4 ohms.
  All 144 completed point averages passed max(0.10 A, 5% of reference):
  mean absolute error 0.03467 A, maximum absolute error 0.11957 A.
  These are candidate shared-calibration results, not the currently installed
  conversion on channels 2-14, and do not establish accuracy outside the
  measured range (approximately 0.11-3.86 A overall).

  Channel 7 could not complete its first 16-ohm/25% point: protection latched
  CHN_OVERCURRENT | RETRY_LOCKOUT (0x09). After shutdown the Tenma reported
  zero current while its selected ADC waveform still averaged 78.1 counts
  (range 74-87). This does not identify the cause of the trip. Do not mask
  it with calibration changes, increase its threshold, or claim a transfer
  pass for this channel. Further automatic tests of channel 7 were excluded.
  Consequently the condition for applying calibration to ALL channels was
  not met: channel-1-only coefficients remain unchanged.

  The selected-waveform diagnostic firmware was installed by signed USB ZIP;
  incremental main.cpp retained the build label Oct 25 2026 22:21:53.
  Final saved state: all 14 control sources unassigned/off, overrides cleared,
  original channel types/configured duties/current limits restored. Tenma
  remains CR at 16 ohms, input OFF, measured current zero. Both ports closed.
  Final diagnostics: 178170135 samples, zero acquisition failures, no latched
  DMA fault. Default STM32 and STM32_adc_dma builds passed; the unchanged
  thirteen DMA/calibration and eight legacy native tests were rerun and passed.

DMA-only default and global PWM calibration, 2026-10-25:

  At the user's request, the same slope/offset now apply to PWM mean telemetry
  on ALL 14 channels, including channel 7 despite its unresolved transfer-test
  exception. This is not an individual calibration or validation of channel 7.
  Digital KILIS calibration, conservative protection conversion, channel limits
  and acquisition-fault shutdown remain unchanged. The coefficients are
  compiled into PWMCurrent.h, not written over per-channel EEPROM records.

  DMA is unconditional in every firmware environment. There is no legacy
  acquisition flag or separate opt-in environment. The default STM32 signed
  ZIP was installed through Cortex's USB updater; installed build label:
  Oct 25 2026 22:46:02. The linked image has no analogRead, ADC polling,
  ADC deinitialisation or synchronous batch acquisition implementation.
  Static RAM is 116244 bytes (88.7%); stack/heap headroom still needs qualification.
  Fourteen reducer/conversion tests and three diagnostics tests passed.

  Installed shared-calibration telemetry was checked on channel 2 at 16 ohms
  and 25/50/75/100% duty. All four means matched the Tenma within the existing
  tolerance and the expected conversion within 0.001 A.
  The original pre-dma-load-test-snapshot.json configuration was then restored
  and saved: channel 1 is digital again with configured PWM duty zero, all 14
  original input assignments restored, and bench overrides cleared. Two
  separate readbacks matched all original configuration sections, excluding
  live telemetry. Original control sources can enable outputs automatically.
  Tenma input was left OFF at 16 ohms because outputs remain externally tied.

  Final post-restoration USB checks encountered intermittent COM18 disappearance
  and I/O errors; a continuous stability check and final acquisition-counter
  read could not be completed. No root cause has been established. Do not
  interpret configuration readback as controller stability qualification.
  The last successful readback had no channel faults or ADC_ERROR, but channel
  7 still reported approximately 1.15 A with the Tenma input off. That sensing
  anomaly and the earlier PWM protection trip remain unresolved.

SD recovery/reset-loop correction, 2026-10-25:

  ST-Link captured a HardFault (CFSR 0x00020000, forced HFSR 0x40000000)
  with MSP 0x2001a8b8, inside static data rather than free stack. Repeated
  stack frames alternated FlushPendingLogBuffer and CloseSDFile. A short/failed
  write entered RecoverSDLogging, whose call to CloseSDFile tried to flush
  the same pending batch again. Recursion exhausted stack before watchdog reset.
  This was not fixed by increasing watchdog timeouts or feeding it in the fault.

  RecoverSDLogging now explicitly discards the failed batch and closes the
  handle without flushing before remounting. Partial writes are not replayed,
  avoiding recursive recovery and duplicate data. A failed batch can lose log
  data; the change does not make a failing SD card healthy.
  Five native mock tests compiled the exact production recovery/flush/close
  function bodies and passed: successful write accounting, zero-byte failure,
  partial failure during close, failed remount and failed reopen.

  The corrected application was programmed and verified through ST-Link at
  0x08010000, leaving bootloader and EEPROM configuration intact. main.cpp's
  incremental build label remains Oct 25 2026 22:46:02; that label alone does
  not distinguish the corrected Storage.cpp image.
  Cortex then received uninterrupted telemetry for 30 seconds (1988 updates).
  Subsequent diagnostics recorded 20990960 ADC samples, zero acquisition
  failures and no latched ADC fault. All original configuration sections
  were checked again after flashing and still matched the pre-test backup.
  The normal watchdog remains enabled.
  A second 30-second Cortex monitor also passed (1965 updates). Final counters
  reached 57856525 samples with zero acquisition failures; SD-card and
  telemetry-offline status were reported, with no ADC fault.

  SD-card error 0x0010 remains reported. Non-halting ST-Link inspection showed
  SD HAL error 0x2024 (RX overrun, command-response timeout and illegal command).
  Its initiating cause is not established. Card logging/recovery needs further
  investigation; do not treat a stable controller as proof of successful logging.
  Do not format or erase the card as part of this recovery verification.
